// Researched, source-linked background for each molecule reference page: what the substance is,
// its state at room temperature, real uses, and safety notes. Written in our own words from the
// cited page (Wikipedia unless noted). "unstable" marks species that cannot be kept at room temperature.
export type MoleculeAbout = { about: string; state: "gas" | "liquid" | "solid" | "ion" | "unstable"; stateNote?: string; source: string };

export const moleculeAbout: Record<string, MoleculeAbout> = {
  "CO₂": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_dioxide",
    about: "Carbon dioxide is a colorless gas that is odorless at ordinary concentrations. It makes up roughly 0.04% of Earth's atmosphere (about 428 ppm in 2025) and is also dissolved in oceans, lakes, and groundwater. It is released by respiration, burning fuels, decay, and volcanoes. Plants, algae, and cyanobacteria take it in during photosynthesis to build sugars and release oxygen, making it the starting point of most food chains. It is also a greenhouse gas: it absorbs infrared radiation and helps set Earth's surface temperature, so its rising level is a key driver of climate change. Industry uses it in food and beverage processing, fertilizer production, oil recovery, cooling, and fire suppression. Safety: breathing air with more than about 5% CO₂ causes breathing problems, and 7–10% can lead to dizziness and unconsciousness." },
  "BF₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boron_trifluoride",
    about: "Boron trifluoride is a colorless, toxic gas with a sharp, pungent odor. Chemists value it as a strong Lewis acid, meaning it readily accepts an electron pair from other molecules, and it is a versatile starting material for making other boron compounds. Its most important use is as a catalyst in organic chemistry, where it promotes reactions such as alkylation, acylation, esterification, isomerization, and the polymerization of unsaturated compounds. It is also used as a boron dopant in semiconductor manufacturing and in neutron detectors. It can be stored as a chilled liquid below its critical temperature of about −12 °C. Safety: BF₃ reacts with moisture to form boric acid and fluoroboric acid, releasing heat; it is corrosive to skin and lungs and must be handled in corrosion-resistant equipment." },
  "CH₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Methane",
    about: "Methane is the simplest hydrocarbon and the main ingredient of natural gas. At room temperature it is a colorless, odorless gas that becomes liquid only when cooled to about −162 °C. It forms both through geological processes and through microbes: wetlands, livestock digestion, seafloor sediments, and decaying organic matter all release it, with wetlands supplying roughly a quarter of atmospheric methane. Most commercial methane comes from natural gas fields and coal seams. It is burned for heating and electricity, is increasingly used as a rocket fuel, and is a raw material for making hydrogen and other chemicals. Methane is a potent greenhouse gas, about 30 times stronger than CO₂ over 100 years, and its atmospheric level has risen about 160% since preindustrial times. Safety: it is extremely flammable, forms explosive mixtures with air, and can displace oxygen in enclosed spaces." },
  "NH₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonia",
    about: "Ammonia is a compound of nitrogen and hydrogen and one of the world's most important industrial chemicals. At room temperature it is a colorless gas, lighter than air, with a sharp, pungent smell; it liquefies easily under pressure and boils at about −33 °C. On Earth it comes from fertilizers, livestock urine, burning biomass, and soil, and it is a common nitrogen waste product of animals. It has even been detected on other planets and in interstellar space. Industry makes it by the Haber–Bosch process, which combines nitrogen from the air with hydrogen; about 235 million tonnes were produced in 2021. Roughly 88% is used for fertilizers, and it also serves as a refrigerant and is being explored as a fuel. Safety: ammonia is toxic and corrosive, and it is classed as an extremely hazardous substance that requires specialized storage." },
  "H₂O": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Properties_of_water",
    about: "Water is the most abundant substance on Earth's surface, found in oceans (which hold about 97% of it), ice caps, lakes, rivers, groundwater, and as vapor in the air. At room temperature it is a tasteless, odorless liquid that looks colorless in a glass but faintly blue in large volumes such as lakes or glaciers, because it weakly absorbs red light. It is notable for occurring naturally on Earth's surface as a solid, a liquid, and a gas. Water is often called the universal solvent because it dissolves many ionic and polar substances, which makes it the medium for the chemistry of life. Two unusual properties shape our planet: ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats and protects aquatic life below, and water's high heat capacity lets it absorb large amounts of heat, moderating Earth's climate." },
  "CS₂": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_disulfide",
    about: "Carbon disulfide is a volatile liquid at room temperature. Pure samples are colorless with a pleasant, ether-like smell, but commercial grades are usually yellowish and smell foul, like rotting radishes, because of impurities. About three quarters of the carbon disulfide produced each year goes into making viscose rayon fibers and cellophane film. It is also an intermediate in manufacturing carbon tetrachloride and sulfur-containing organic compounds, including chemicals used in mineral flotation to separate ores. Safety: it is extremely flammable, with a flash point of −43 °C and an autoignition temperature of only about 102 °C, so even hot surfaces can ignite its vapor. It is also a nerve poison: heavy exposure can cause neurological and psychiatric effects, and long-term workplace exposure has been linked to hearing and vision problems and higher rates of stroke." },
  "BeF₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beryllium_fluoride",
    about: "Beryllium fluoride is a white solid at room temperature, usually seen as colorless, glassy lumps. In the solid, each beryllium is bonded to four fluoride ions in a network resembling cristobalite, a form of silica, so discrete BeF₂ molecules exist only in the gas phase. Unlike quartz, however, it dissolves readily in water. It is the principal starting material for making beryllium metal, which is produced by heating it with magnesium at about 1300 °C. Mixed with lithium fluoride it forms the molten salt FLiBe, a favored coolant for liquid-fluoride nuclear reactor designs because of its low melting point and good neutron properties. Biochemists also use it to mimic phosphate when crystallizing proteins. Safety: beryllium fluoride is highly toxic, and workplace exposure to beryllium compounds is strictly limited." },
  "BeCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beryllium_chloride",
    about: "Beryllium chloride is a white to pale yellow crystalline solid at room temperature that readily absorbs moisture from the air. It melts at 399 °C and boils at 482 °C. In the solid, beryllium atoms are linked by bridging chloride ions into a polymer, so the isolated BeCl₂ molecule shown here exists only in the hot vapor, where it occurs alongside a chloride-bridged dimer. In many ways it behaves like aluminum chloride, and it dissolves easily in polar solvents. It is used as a raw material for producing beryllium metal by electrolysis and as a catalyst for Friedel–Crafts reactions in organic chemistry. With water it forms a tetrahydrate containing [Be(H₂O)₄]²⁺ ions. Safety: like other beryllium compounds it is toxic, and workplace exposure limits are very strict." },
  "BeBr₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beryllium_bromide",
    about: "Beryllium bromide is a colorless to white crystalline solid at room temperature. It is made by reacting beryllium metal directly with bromine at 500–700 °C. In the solid, beryllium centers are joined by bridging bromide ions into an extended inorganic polymer rather than separate molecules, so the simple BeBr₂ unit shown here does not describe the crystal; the solid sublimes at about 473 °C. The small, highly charged Be²⁺ ion makes the compound a strong Lewis acid, and with diethyl ether it forms a colorless complex. It is very hygroscopic and dissolves well in water, where it slowly hydrolyzes to beryllium hydroxide and hydrogen bromide. Safety: like all beryllium compounds it is toxic if inhaled or swallowed, and exposure is strictly regulated." },
  "MgCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnesium_chloride",
    about: "Magnesium chloride is a white or colorless salt that is very soluble in water. It commonly occurs as hydrates, and the solid is deliquescent: it can absorb so much moisture from humid air that it dissolves into a liquid. In the solid, Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions form an ionic crystal lattice rather than individual molecules. It is abundant in nature, dissolved in seawater and in brines such as those of the Great Salt Lake and the Dead Sea, and it occurs as the mineral bischofite in ancient seabed deposits. Anhydrous magnesium chloride is the main raw material for making magnesium metal. It is also spread on roads for de-icing and dust control, and it is the traditional tofu coagulant known in Japan as nigari. Safety: it has low toxicity but can act as an irritant." },
  "MgBr₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnesium_bromide",
    about: "Magnesium bromide is a white, deliquescent solid that absorbs moisture from the air. It exists in anhydrous form and as several hydrates; the common hexahydrate forms colorless crystals built from [Mg(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions and bromide ions. It is extremely soluble in water and moderately soluble in alcohols. It occurs naturally in some minerals, such as bischofite and carnallite. In organic chemistry it serves as a Lewis acid catalyst, for example in aldol reactions, and it is used to modify palladium catalysts for hydrogenation. In the past it was given as a tranquilizer and anticonvulsant, and the hexahydrate has flame-retardant properties. Safety: it is considered only a mild hazard, though ordinary laboratory precautions still apply." },
  "ZnCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinc_chloride",
    about: "Zinc chloride is a white, odorless crystalline solid that is strongly hygroscopic and deliquescent: it pulls water from the air and dissolves in it, so it must be stored tightly sealed. It melts at 290 °C, is very soluble in water, and forms several hydrates. A related zinc hydroxychloride occurs in nature as the mineral simonkolleite. Zinc chloride is widely used as a flux in soldering and galvanizing metals, and it also appears in dry-cell batteries, textile processing, fireproofing, and wood preservation. Chemists use it as a Lewis acid catalyst in organic synthesis. Safety: the anhydrous solid is corrosive and can burn skin and other tissue, swallowing it requires medical attention, and zinc chloride smoke from military smoke devices is harmful to breathe." },
  "ZnBr₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinc_bromide",
    about: "Zinc bromide is a white crystalline powder at room temperature and shares many properties with zinc chloride. It is hygroscopic, readily absorbing moisture from the air to form a dihydrate, and it is very soluble in water. Its largest use is in the oil and gas industry: dense zinc bromide brines are pumped into wells during completion and workover operations to displace drilling mud. Solutions of it held between glass panes serve as radiation-shielding windows in hot cells used to handle radioactive material, and unlike lead glass they do not darken permanently from radiation damage. It is also the electrolyte in zinc–bromine batteries and a Lewis acid in laboratory organic chemistry. Safety: its brines are corrosive and dehydrating to skin, so protective equipment is needed, and swallowing a few grams can be toxic." },
  "CdCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadmium_chloride",
    about: "Cadmium chloride is a white crystalline solid that is hygroscopic and highly soluble in water, though only slightly soluble in alcohol. Its layered crystal arrangement is so characteristic that crystallographers use the \"cadmium chloride structure\" as a reference type when describing other compounds, so the solid is an extended lattice rather than separate CdCl₂ molecules. Its main use is preparing cadmium sulfide, the basis of the brilliant, stable pigment cadmium yellow, and it has also been used in electroplating and photocopying. Chemists once used it to make organocadmium reagents, but less toxic alternatives have largely replaced them. It was also used in early experiments to detect neutrinos. Safety: cadmium chloride is highly toxic and is classified as a known human carcinogen (IARC Group 1), so it requires strict handling precautions." },
  "HgCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury(II)_chloride",
    about: "Mercury(II) chloride, historically called corrosive sublimate or mercuric chloride, is a colorless or white, odorless crystalline solid. Unlike most metal chlorides it is made of discrete HgCl₂ molecules rather than ions, which is why it sublimes readily when heated. It has a long history of use: it treated syphilis before antibiotics, served as a wound disinfectant, intensified early photographs, and protected museum specimens and wood from insects, mites, and mold. Its main industrial role has been as a catalyst for converting acetylene into vinyl chloride, although that process has largely been replaced. It is still used as a reagent in organic synthesis and chemical analysis and to sterilize the surfaces of plant tissue for culture. Safety: it is highly toxic, damaging the digestive tract and kidneys, and severe poisoning can be fatal within 24 hours." },
  "N₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azide",
    about: "The azide ion is an anion made of three nitrogen atoms carrying a −1 charge. It is the conjugate base of hydrazoic acid (HN₃) and is found in salts such as sodium azide, lead azide, silver azide, and barium azide, and as a group in organic azides. Sodium azide is the most important industrial azide: it decomposes rapidly to nitrogen gas, which has been used to inflate automobile airbags, and in laboratories it is added to solutions as a preservative to stop bacterial growth. Lead azide and other heavy-metal azides are shock-sensitive explosives used in detonators. Organic azides are key reagents in click chemistry, reacting with alkynes to form triazoles. Safety: sodium azide is nearly as toxic as sodium cyanide; acids convert azides into toxic, volatile hydrazoic acid, and heavy-metal azides can explode when heated or shaken." },
  "BCl₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boron_trichloride",
    about: "Boron trichloride is a colorless gas at room temperature that condenses to a liquid just below 12.6 °C. It is a strong Lewis acid that forms adducts with amines, phosphines, and ethers, and it is a useful reagent in organic synthesis, where it breaks carbon–oxygen bonds in ethers. Industry uses it as a starting material for producing elemental boron, in plasma etching during semiconductor manufacturing, and in refining metal alloys and as a soldering flux. Safety: boron trichloride reacts violently with water to form boric acid and hydrochloric acid, and it forms hydrogen chloride on contact with moist air. It can cause serious burns to the eyes, skin, mouth, and lungs, and it may be fatal if inhaled or swallowed, so it is handled only with dry equipment and good ventilation." },
  "BBr₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boron_tribromide",
    about: "Boron tribromide is a dense, fuming liquid at room temperature. Pure samples are colorless, but commercial material is often amber to reddish-brown because of bromine contamination. It is a very strong Lewis acid. Its best-known use, in research labs and in pharmaceutical manufacturing, is cleaving ethers, especially removing methyl groups from molecules in a step called demethylation. The semiconductor industry uses it as a boron source for doping, and it also catalyzes Friedel–Crafts reactions and olefin polymerization. Safety: BBr₃ reacts violently with water, alcohols, and the metals sodium and potassium, and it attacks metals, wood, and rubber. It is acutely toxic and corrosive to skin and eyes, so it must be kept dry and handled with protective equipment in a fume hood." },
  "BI₃": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boron_triiodide",
    about: "Boron triiodide is a crystalline solid at room temperature that melts at about 50 °C. Pure crystals are colorless and shiny, but samples often look reddish. It can be made by heating boron with iodine at about 210 °C, by reacting boron trichloride with hydrogen iodide at high temperature, or by treating lithium borohydride with iodine. Like the other boron trihalides it is a strong Lewis acid, and it is used to produce other chemicals and as a catalyst for coal liquefaction. It is sensitive to both air and moisture, and it decomposes in water to form boric acid and hydroiodic acid. Safety: it is rated a serious health hazard, and because it reacts with moisture it must be kept away from air and water and handled with protective equipment." },
  "AlCl₃": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium_chloride",
    about: "Aluminum chloride in its anhydrous form is a white or colorless crystalline solid, although commercial samples often look yellow from traces of iron(III) chloride. Its structure changes with temperature: the solid is an extended ionic-like lattice, the melt contains Al₂Cl₆ dimers, and only at higher temperatures do those dimers split into the single AlCl₃ molecules shown here. It is one of the most important Lewis acid catalysts in chemistry, used on a large scale for Friedel–Crafts alkylation and acylation of aromatic compounds. Its hexahydrate is applied to the skin to treat hyperhidrosis, or excessive sweating. Safety: the anhydrous solid is corrosive and strongly hygroscopic; it fumes in moist air and hisses when added to water, releasing hydrogen chloride, so it must be kept dry and handled with care." },
  "AlF₃": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium_fluoride",
    about: "Aluminum fluoride is a colorless to white, odorless crystalline solid with a very high melting point of about 1,290 °C. In the solid, aluminum and fluoride ions are linked into a three-dimensional network, which explains that high melting point; separate AlF₃ molecules exist only when the compound is vaporized. It occurs naturally in rare minerals: the trihydrate as rosenbergite and the anhydrous form as óskarssonite. Its main use is in the aluminum industry, where it is added to the molten salt bath used for electrolytic aluminum production, lowering the bath's melting point and increasing its electrical conductivity. Safety: aluminum fluoride is corrosive and acutely toxic, and long-term inhalation of its dust may cause asthma as well as fluoride-related effects on bones and the nervous system." },
  "GaCl₃": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallium(III)_chloride",
    about: "Gallium trichloride, also called gallium(III) chloride, is a colorless solid that forms needle-like crystals and absorbs moisture from the air. In the solid it exists as Ga₂Cl₆ pairs held together by bridging chlorine atoms, so the single GaCl₃ molecule shown here does not describe the crystal. Because the solid is made of discrete molecules, it melts at only about 78 °C, far lower than typical metal chlorides. It is the main starting material for other gallium compounds, including trimethylgallium used in semiconductor manufacturing, and it serves as a Lewis acid catalyst for Friedel–Crafts reactions. About 110 tons of gallium chloride solution filled the GALLEX and GNO detectors that counted neutrinos from the Sun. Safety: it dissolves in water with heat release to give an acidic solution, and it is highly corrosive, causing severe skin burns and eye damage." },
  "SO₃": { state: "solid", stateNote: "a fuming solid or liquid near room temperature (one form melts at about 17 °C)", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_trioxide",
    about: "Sulfur trioxide is the most economically important sulfur oxide, produced on a huge scale as the key intermediate in making sulfuric acid. It is made by the contact process, in which sulfur dioxide is oxidized over a vanadium pentoxide catalyst at 400–600 °C. Near room temperature it is a colorless to white substance that fumes in air and exists in several forms: single SO₃ molecules in the gas, a trimer that melts at about 17 °C, and polymeric solids that melt at higher temperatures. It is also used to make sulfonate surfactants for detergents. In the atmosphere, gaseous SO₃ is the main precursor of acid rain. Safety: it reacts violently with water, releasing a great deal of heat, and is a powerful corrosive and dehydrating agent that can ignite organic materials." },
  "CO₃²⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbonate",
    about: "The carbonate ion is a polyatomic anion made of one carbon atom and three oxygen atoms with an overall charge of −2. It is found in a huge range of salts and minerals, including calcium carbonate (calcite, the main component of limestone), dolomite (calcium magnesium carbonate), and siderite, an important iron ore. The soluble salts sodium carbonate and potassium carbonate have long been used for cleaning and glassmaking. Mollusks and corals build shells and skeletons from calcium carbonate. In water, carbonate takes part in a linked equilibrium with bicarbonate, carbonic acid, and dissolved carbon dioxide. This system buffers seawater, plays a major role in the long-term carbon cycle and climate, and, through reactions involving bicarbonate, helps keep human blood pH between about 7.37 and 7.43." },
  "NO₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrate",
    about: "The nitrate ion is a polyatomic anion of one nitrogen and three oxygen atoms with a −1 charge. It occurs in salts such as potassium nitrate, ammonium nitrate, and sodium nitrate (historically called Chile saltpeter), and nearly all inorganic nitrates dissolve readily in water. Nitrate salts are key nitrogen fertilizers for crops, and soil bacteria also produce nitrate naturally during nitrification, part of the nitrogen cycle. Because its nitrogen is in the +5 oxidation state, nitrate can act as a strong oxidizer, which is why nitrates are used in gunpowder and other explosives; they are also used to cure meats. Their high solubility has a downside: fertilizer runoff pollutes waterways and can create oxygen-poor dead zones, and nitrate reduced to nitrite can cause methemoglobinemia in young infants. Safety: solid nitrates are oxidizers that can intensify fires." },
  "BO₃³⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borate",
    about: "The orthoborate ion is the simplest borate anion: one boron atom bonded to three oxygen atoms, carrying a −3 charge. It appears as a distinct unit in solid orthoborate salts, such as trisodium orthoborate (Na₃BO₃) and magnesium borate, Mg₃(BO₃)₂. The free ion does not persist in ordinary water. Instead, dissolved boron is found mainly as boric acid, B(OH)₃, a weak acid that accepts a hydroxide ion to become tetrahydroxyborate, B(OH)₄⁻, along with larger polymeric borate ions whose balance depends on pH. So in everyday chemistry, the BO₃³⁻ ion is best thought of as a building block of solid borate compounds rather than something found in solution. Borates as a family are commercially important, used in glassmaking, in detergents, and as flame retardants." },
  "SO₂": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_dioxide",
    about: "Sulfur dioxide is a colorless gas with a sharp, choking odor often compared to a freshly struck match. It boils at about −10 °C, so it is easily liquefied by cooling or mild pressure. Volcanoes release it naturally, and burning sulfur-containing fuels adds much more in industrial regions, where it is a major air pollutant and a contributor to acid rain. Beyond Earth, it makes up most of the thin atmosphere of Jupiter's moon Io. Its biggest industrial role is as the intermediate in making sulfuric acid by the contact process. It is also a food preservative (E220) for dried fruit and is added to wine as an antioxidant and antimicrobial. Breathing it irritates the airways, and people with asthma can be especially sensitive." },
  "O₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ozone",
    about: "Ozone is a form of oxygen made of three oxygen atoms instead of the usual two, and it is far less stable and much more reactive than ordinary O₂. At room temperature it is a pale blue gas with a sharp smell a bit like chlorine bleach, noticeable even at around 0.01 ppm. High in the stratosphere, the ozone layer absorbs much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet light. Near the ground, however, ozone forms from sunlight-driven reactions of other pollutants and is a key part of smog. Because it is such a strong oxidizer, it is used to disinfect and treat drinking water, for bleaching, and in ozonolysis reactions that split carbon–carbon double bonds. Above roughly 0.1 ppm it damages the lungs and irritates the eyes and throat." },
  "NO₂⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrite",
    about: "The nitrite ion is a negatively charged ion of one nitrogen and two oxygen atoms. It is best known in salts such as sodium nitrite and potassium nitrite, which dissolve in water to release the ion. In nature, nitrite is a key intermediate in the nitrogen cycle: soil bacteria such as Nitrosomonas oxidize ammonium to nitrite, and Nitrobacter then converts nitrite to nitrate. Sodium nitrite is widely used to cure processed meats, where it fixes the pink color, adds flavor, and slows bacterial growth. Chemists also use nitrite to make diazonium salts for azo dyes, and nitrites are part of cyanide-poisoning antidote kits. Swallowing large amounts can cause methemoglobinemia, and nitrite in cured meats can form nitrosamines linked to cancer." },
  "SnCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin(II)_chloride",
    about: "Tin(II) chloride, also called stannous chloride or \"tin salt,\" is a white, odorless crystalline solid that readily forms a stable dihydrate, SnCl₂·2H₂O. The isolated bent SnCl₂ molecule is found in the gas phase; in the solid, SnCl₂ units link into chains through bridging chloride atoms. It is a useful reducing agent, especially in acidic solution, where it reduces silver, gold, and iron(III) salts. Acidic SnCl₂ solutions are used to electroplate tin onto steel for tin cans. It also serves as a mordant in textile dyeing and as a food additive (E512) that helps canned and bottled foods keep their color. It is corrosive and irritating to skin and the respiratory tract, and it is harmful to aquatic life." },
  "SnBr₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin(II)_bromide",
    about: "Tin(II) bromide is a compound of tin in its +2 oxidation state with bromine. At room temperature it is a yellow powder that melts at about 215 °C. The individual bent SnBr₂ molecule exists in the gas phase, with a Br–Sn–Br angle of about 95°; in the solid, each tin atom is surrounded by several bromine neighbors and the units join into extended chains. It can be made by dissolving tin metal in hydrobromic acid and then removing the water and excess acid; if oxygen is present, tin(IV) bromide forms instead. In the lab it acts as a reducing agent and as a Lewis acid that forms adducts with electron-pair donors. It is classified as corrosive and should be handled with eye and skin protection." },
  "PbCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead(II)_chloride",
    about: "Lead(II) chloride is a white, odorless solid that forms needle-like crystals and occurs naturally as the mineral cotunnite. It dissolves only slightly in cold water (about 1 g per 100 mL at 20 °C) but becomes more soluble in hot water or concentrated hydrochloric acid. The bent PbCl₂ molecule, with a Cl–Pb–Cl angle near 98°, exists in the gas phase; in the solid, each lead ion is surrounded by nine chloride ions in a crystal lattice. Its low solubility in cold water is used in qualitative analysis to separate and identify lead ions by precipitation. It is also used to make infrared-transmitting glass and was historically used in white pigments. Like other lead compounds, it can cause lead poisoning if ingested or inhaled." },
  "GeCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanium_dichloride",
    about: "Germanium dichloride is a compound of germanium in its uncommon +2 oxidation state. In bulk it is a white to pale yellow solid, but the individual GeCl₂ molecule, called dichlorogermylene, is a short-lived, highly reactive species that inserts into many kinds of chemical bonds. It is made in the lab by heating germanium tetrachloride with germanium metal at about 300 °C, by decomposing trichlorogermane, or by reducing GeCl₄ with hydrogen at high temperature. Because the free molecule is so reactive, chemists usually work with a more stable complex with dioxane, GeCl₂·dioxane, as a convenient source of GeCl₂ in synthesis. With water it hydrolyzes to germanium(II) hydroxide, which breaks down to germanium monoxide. It is mainly a research chemical rather than a commercial product." },
  "GeF₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanium_difluoride",
    about: "Germanium difluoride is a compound of germanium in the +2 oxidation state with fluorine. At room temperature it forms white crystals that readily absorb moisture from the air. It melts at about 110 °C and sublimes near 130 °C. In the solid, the compound is not made of separate GeF₂ molecules: it is built from polymeric chains of linked GeF₃ pyramids, with some fluorine atoms weakly bridging neighboring chains. The simple bent molecule shown here is an idealized, isolated unit. Germanium difluoride can be prepared by heating germanium tetrafluoride with germanium powder at 150–300 °C. It is mainly of research interest. The key hazard is that it reacts with water to release hydrogen fluoride, a highly corrosive and toxic acid, so it must be kept dry." },
  "CCl₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_tetrachloride",
    about: "Carbon tetrachloride, also called tetrachloromethane or \"carbon tet,\" is a dense, colorless, nonflammable liquid with a sweet odor similar to chloroform. It boils at about 77 °C and evaporates easily. From the 1890s through the mid-1900s it was widely used as a dry-cleaning solvent and in fire extinguishers, and large amounts were converted into the CFC refrigerants R-11 and R-12. Because it depletes stratospheric ozone and is a greenhouse gas, its production has dropped sharply since the 1980s under the Montreal Protocol. It is highly toxic: it is one of the most potent liver poisons known, also damages the kidneys, and is classified as possibly carcinogenic to humans." },
  "CF₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrafluoromethane",
    about: "Carbon tetrafluoride, or tetrafluoromethane, is the simplest perfluorocarbon, the fully fluorinated version of methane. It is also known by refrigerant names such as R-14 and PFC-14. At room temperature it is a colorless, odorless gas that is extremely chemically stable. It is used as a low-temperature refrigerant and, in electronics manufacturing, as a plasma etching gas for silicon, silicon dioxide, and silicon nitride. Large amounts are released as a byproduct of aluminum smelting. Although it does not harm the ozone layer, it is an extremely potent greenhouse gas that lasts about 50,000 years in the atmosphere and traps about 6,500 times as much heat as the same mass of CO₂. Its main direct hazard is suffocation, since it can displace air in closed spaces." },
  "CBr₄": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_tetrabromide",
    about: "Carbon tetrabromide, or tetrabromomethane, is a compound of one carbon atom and four bromine atoms. At room temperature it is a solid that forms colorless to yellow-brown crystals with a sweet odor, and it melts at about 94.5 °C. In organic chemistry it is a common reagent: combined with triphenylphosphine it converts alcohols into alkyl bromides in the Appel reaction, and it is used in the Corey–Fuchs reaction to turn aldehydes into alkynes. It has also been used as a solvent for greases, waxes, and oils, in plastic and rubber manufacturing, and in fire-resistant formulations because it does not burn. It is toxic and irritating, with a workplace exposure limit of only 0.1 ppm, so it should be handled with good ventilation and protective gear." },
  "SiH₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silane",
    about: "Silane is the simplest silicon hydride, the silicon counterpart of methane. At room temperature it is a colorless gas with a sharp, unpleasant smell somewhat like vinegar. Unlike methane, silane is pyrophoric: it can ignite on its own when it contacts air. Its main use is in the semiconductor industry, where it is decomposed to deposit thin layers of very pure silicon for computer chips. It is also used to make hydrogenated amorphous silicon for solar panels, and silane-based compounds serve as coupling agents that help coatings stick to surfaces. Silane is dangerous to handle: leaks have caused fatal industrial fires and explosions, and it is also mildly toxic, with a recommended workplace exposure limit of 5 ppm." },
  "SiCl₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_tetrachloride",
    about: "Silicon tetrachloride, also called tetrachlorosilane, is a colorless, volatile liquid that boils at about 58 °C. Its low boiling point makes it easy to purify by distillation, which is why it is an important intermediate in making ultrapure polysilicon for semiconductors and solar cells. High-purity SiCl₄ is also used to make optical fibers, and reacting it with water in a controlled way produces fumed silica. When the liquid meets moist air, it reacts to form a white, cloud-like smoke of silica and hydrochloric acid. It is irritating to the skin and eyes, and the acid fumes it releases make it hazardous to handle outside dry conditions." },
  "SiF₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_tetrafluoride",
    about: "Silicon tetrafluoride, or tetrafluorosilane, is a colorless gas at room temperature with an unusually narrow liquid range: its boiling point is only about 4 °C above its melting point. It forms whenever hydrofluoric acid attacks silica or silicate minerals, which is why it is a major byproduct of phosphate fertilizer production. It also occurs naturally in volcanic plumes, which can release several tonnes per day, and in gases from coal fires. In water it partly hydrolyzes to form hexafluorosilicic acid. It is used in microelectronics and in organic synthesis, and has been studied as a raw material for making polycrystalline silicon. Silicon tetrafluoride is toxic and corrosive, can be fatal if inhaled, and causes severe burns to skin and eyes." },
  "SiBr₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_tetrabromide",
    about: "Silicon tetrabromide, also called tetrabromosilane or silicon(IV) bromide, is a colorless liquid at room temperature with a suffocating odor. It melts at about 5 °C and boils at about 153 °C. It reacts readily with moisture: exposed to air, it fumes as it hydrolyzes into silicon dioxide and hydrogen bromide. Its main use is as a starting material for silicon nitride coatings, made by heating it and treating it with ammonia; silicon nitride is used in ceramics, sealants, and cutting tools. In practice, the cheaper and more widely available silicon tetrachloride is usually chosen for the same jobs. Silicon tetrabromide is corrosive and harmful if swallowed, inhaled, or spilled on skin, and it can cause serious eye damage." },
  "GeH₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germane",
    about: "Germane is the simplest germanium hydride, the germanium counterpart of methane. At room temperature it is a colorless gas with a pungent odor that boils at about −88 °C and dissolves only slightly in water. It has been detected in the atmosphere of Jupiter. Germane breaks down when heated to around 600 K (about 330 °C), and this instability is exactly what makes it useful: the semiconductor industry uses it to grow thin crystalline layers of germanium on chips by vapor-phase deposition methods. Germane is highly flammable, possibly pyrophoric, and highly toxic, with a workplace exposure limit of just 0.2 ppm. Exposure can cause headache, dizziness, nausea, kidney injury, and damage to red blood cells." },
  "GeCl₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanium_tetrachloride",
    about: "Germanium tetrachloride is a colorless, fuming liquid with an acidic smell. It melts at about −49.5 °C and boils at about 86.5 °C. It is a key intermediate in refining germanium: germanium dioxide is dissolved in concentrated hydrochloric acid, and the resulting GeCl₄ is purified by fractional distillation before being converted back to pure germanium. Its biggest modern use is in making optical fibers. GeCl₄ is fed with oxygen into the glass preform, where it is oxidized to germanium dioxide, which raises the glass's refractive index and lets manufacturers control how light travels through the fiber. It slowly reacts with water to form hydrochloric acid and germanium dioxide. It is corrosive and a lachrymator, meaning its vapor makes the eyes tear." },
  "GeF₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanium_tetrafluoride",
    about: "Germanium tetrafluoride is a compound of one germanium atom and four fluorine atoms. At room temperature it is a colorless gas with a garlic-like odor; it sublimes at about −36.5 °C instead of forming a liquid at normal pressure. It can be made by reacting germanium directly with fluorine gas, by treating germanium dioxide with hydrofluoric acid, or by heating barium hexafluorogermanate. In the semiconductor industry, it is used together with disilane to produce silicon–germanium (SiGe), a material used in high-performance electronics. It is very sensitive to moisture and reacts with water to form germanium dioxide and hydrofluoric acid. Because it releases hydrofluoric acid on contact with moisture, it is corrosive and toxic." },
  "SnCl₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin(IV)_chloride",
    about: "Tin(IV) chloride, also called stannic chloride, is a colorless liquid that strongly absorbs moisture and fumes when exposed to air. It was first described in the 1500s by the alchemist Andreas Libavius and was once known as \"spirit of Libavius.\" With water it forms a solid pentahydrate, SnCl₄·5H₂O, historically called \"butter of tin.\" Chemists value it as a Lewis acid catalyst for Friedel–Crafts reactions and nitrations, and it is a starting material for organotin compounds used as catalysts and as stabilizers for plastics. During World War I, its irritating fumes were used in chemical warfare. It is corrosive, causing severe skin burns and eye damage, and it is harmful to aquatic life, so it must be kept dry and handled carefully." },
  "SnBr₄": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin(IV)_bromide",
    about: "Tin(IV) bromide, also called stannic bromide or tin tetrabromide, is a compound of tin in its +4 oxidation state with bromine. At room temperature it is a colorless to pale yellow crystalline solid with an unusually low melting point of about 31 °C, so it can melt on a warm day, and it boils at about 205 °C. It can be made by reacting tin metal directly with bromine. It dissolves readily in water, where it forms a series of tin complexes containing bromide and water. It is sensitive to air and moisture, causes severe skin burns, and irritates the respiratory tract." },
  "TiCl₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titanium_tetrachloride",
    about: "Titanium tetrachloride, nicknamed \"tickle\" or \"tickle 4\" after the sound of its formula, is a colorless liquid at room temperature, though impure samples look yellow or brown. It is one of the few transition metal halides that is a liquid under ordinary conditions. It is a crucial industrial intermediate: in the Kroll process it is reduced with magnesium to make titanium metal, and most of the TiCl₄ produced is converted into titanium dioxide, a widely used white pigment. It is also used to make Ziegler–Natta catalysts for producing polymers. In humid air it gives off thick white clouds of titanium dioxide and hydrochloric acid, which is why it was once used for smoke screens and movie smoke. It is toxic and corrosive." },
  "NH₄⁺": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonium",
    about: "The ammonium ion is a positively charged ion made of one nitrogen atom and four hydrogen atoms. It forms when ammonia accepts a proton (H⁺) from an acid, and its solutions are mildly acidic. It is found in common salts such as ammonium chloride, ammonium nitrate, and ammonium sulfate, which are important components of fertilizers. Ammonium plays a central role in the nitrogen cycle: plants reduce absorbed nitrate to ammonium and build it into amino acids. In animals, ammonium is a nitrogen-containing waste product. Fish and other aquatic animals excrete it directly, mammals convert it into urea, and birds turn it into uric acid to save water. If it builds up in the blood, a condition called hyperammonemia, it becomes toxic." },
  "PO₄³⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphate",
    about: "The phosphate ion is a triply charged negative ion made of one phosphorus atom and four oxygen atoms, derived from phosphoric acid. It occurs naturally in minerals such as apatite, and large phosphate rock deposits in places like Florida, Morocco, and China are mined as the main source. Your bones and teeth are built largely from a calcium phosphate mineral called hydroxyapatite. Phosphate is also essential to life at the molecular level: phosphate groups form the sugar-phosphate backbone of DNA and RNA, and adding or removing phosphate in ATP and ADP drives cellular energy use. Phosphates are major ingredients in fertilizers and are also used in detergents and foods. Too much phosphate in lakes and rivers can cause eutrophication, where algal blooms lead to oxygen depletion." },
  "SO₄²⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfate",
    about: "The sulfate ion is a doubly charged negative ion made of one sulfur atom and four oxygen atoms. It is found in many common minerals and salts. Gypsum, a hydrated calcium sulfate, is mined on a huge scale for plaster and construction materials. Epsom salt is magnesium sulfate, and medical magnesium sulfate is used to treat eclampsia in pregnancy. Barium sulfate (the mineral barite) is so insoluble that it is useful in medical imaging. Copper sulfate is used as an agricultural fungicide, and sulfate chemicals are central to the kraft process for making paper pulp. In the atmosphere, tiny sulfate particles reflect sunlight and affect climate. Some bacteria in oxygen-free sediments use sulfate instead of oxygen to obtain energy." },
  "ClO₄⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perchlorate",
    about: "The perchlorate ion is a negatively charged ion made of one chlorine atom and four oxygen atoms. It is found in salts such as ammonium perchlorate, potassium perchlorate, and sodium perchlorate, and in perchloric acid. Perchlorates occur naturally in the nitrate deposits of Chile's Atacama Desert and were detected at about 0.6% by weight in Martian soil. Their main use is as powerful oxidizers in solid rocket propellants and fireworks; they are also used to generate oxygen aboard spacecraft. Perchlorate is very soluble in water and persists for decades, and it has contaminated groundwater in many U.S. states. In the body, it blocks the thyroid's uptake of iodide, so high exposure can disrupt thyroid function." },
  "BF₄⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrafluoroborate",
    about: "The tetrafluoroborate ion is a negatively charged ion made of one boron atom surrounded by four fluorine atoms. It occurs in salts such as sodium tetrafluoroborate, silver tetrafluoroborate, and nitrosonium tetrafluoroborate, and in fluoroboric acid. It can be made by combining boron trifluoride with fluoride salts, or boric acid with hydrofluoric acid. Chemists prize it as a \"weakly coordinating\" anion: its charge is spread evenly over four highly electronegative fluorine atoms, so it rarely interferes with the positive ion it accompanies. It is often chosen instead of perchlorate because it does not form explosive salts. Tetrafluoroborates are used in electroplating, as electrolytes, as building blocks for ionic liquids, in fluxes, and in the Balz–Schiemann reaction, which makes aryl fluorides from diazonium salts." },
  "MnO₄⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permanganate",
    about: "Permanganate is the manganate(VII) ion, an oxyanion in which manganese sits in its +7 oxidation state. It is best known from potassium permanganate, KMnO₄, although sodium, calcium, barium and ammonium salts also exist. Dissolved in water, the ion gives an intense purple color that comes from charge-transfer absorption between oxygen and manganese. Permanganate is a powerful oxidizer: in acid it is reduced to manganese(II), in neutral solution to manganese dioxide, and in base to manganate. Chemists use it for redox titrations (permanganometry) and as an oxidizing reagent in organic synthesis, while it is also applied as a disinfectant and as a water-treatment additive in aquaculture. Handle solid permanganate with care: it is a strong oxidizer, and swallowing roughly 10 g can be fatal, damaging mucous membranes, the liver and the kidneys." },
  "CrO₄²⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromate_and_dichromate",
    about: "Chromate is an oxyanion of chromium in the +6 (hexavalent) oxidation state. Its salts, such as potassium chromate and sodium chromate, are bright yellow solids, and their water solutions are yellow too. In solution chromate is linked to dichromate by an acid–base equilibrium: basic conditions favor chromate, while adding acid converts it to dichromate. The most common chromate mineral in nature is crocoite, lead chromate, which can form striking long red crystals. Chromates have been used in chrome plating and corrosion protection of metals, as pigments such as the lead-based chrome yellow, and as oxidizing agents that are reduced to blue-green chromium(III) salts. Hexavalent chromium compounds are carcinogens; breathing in their dust is linked to lung and nasal cancers, and their use in consumer goods is now restricted." },
  "XeO₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenon_tetroxide",
    about: "Xenon tetroxide is one of the few compounds of a noble gas, and the xenon in it holds the +8 oxidation state, the highest xenon can reach; only oxygen is able to push xenon this far. It is made in the lab by converting a perxenate salt into perxenic acid with sulfuric acid and then dehydrating that acid. Below −35.9 °C it is a yellow crystalline solid, and its listed boiling point is about 0 °C, so it would be a gas at room temperature, but in practice it is badly unstable above its melting point. It has no commercial uses and is studied only as a research chemical. Safety note: xenon tetroxide is a powerful explosive that tends to decompose violently into xenon and oxygen, so it must be kept and handled at very low temperatures." },
  "PH₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphine",
    about: "Phosphine is the simplest hydride of phosphorus, a colorless, flammable gas at room temperature. Pure phosphine has no smell, but technical-grade samples reek of rotting fish because of impurities such as diphosphane (P₂H₄), and those same traces can make the gas ignite by itself in air. Industrially it is produced by reacting white phosphorus with sodium or potassium hydroxide. Phosphine is used as a fumigant for pest control, since it breaks down to harmless phosphoric acid residues, and the semiconductor industry uses it as a dopant and as a precursor for compound semiconductors like gallium phosphide. A 2020 report of phosphine in Venus's clouds drew wide attention but was later disputed. Phosphine is a highly toxic respiratory poison, considered immediately dangerous to life or health at 50 ppm." },
  "AsH₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsine",
    about: "Arsine is the simplest hydride of arsenic, a colorless, flammable gas that is denser than air and only slightly soluble in water. The gas itself is odorless, but as it oxidizes in air it can give off a faint garlic- or fish-like smell at concentrations above about 0.5 ppm. Its main modern use is in the microelectronics industry, where it serves as an arsenic source for growing gallium arsenide and related semiconductors by chemical vapor deposition. Arsine also has a place in forensic history: the Marsh test turned arsenic in a sample into arsine, which was then heated to leave a telltale arsenic deposit. Arsine is extremely toxic because it destroys red blood cells; about 250 ppm is rapidly fatal, and it is also a serious fire and explosion hazard." },
  "SbH₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stibine",
    about: "Stibine, also called antimony hydride, is a colorless gas with a garlic-like odor. It is unstable, decomposing slowly at room temperature and quickly at about 200 °C; that decomposition speeds itself up and can become explosive. The semiconductor industry uses stibine in chemical vapor deposition to add small amounts of antimony to silicon as a dopant. Like arsine, it can be detected with the historic Marsh test, where a black mirror of antimony forms in the cool part of the apparatus. Stibine is highly toxic: it attacks red blood cells, so early symptoms such as headache, dizziness and nausea can be followed by hemolytic anemia and kidney damage. It should only be handled with proper gas-handling equipment and ventilation." },
  "NF₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrogen_trifluoride",
    about: "Nitrogen trifluoride is a colorless, nonflammable, toxic gas with a slightly musty smell. At ordinary temperatures it is a sluggish oxidizer, reacting vigorously only when heated, which lets it be stored in contact with many metals and plastics. The electronics industry is its biggest user: it is used to etch silicon during the manufacture of chips, flat-panel displays and thin-film solar cells, and to clean the chambers of plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition tools. It also supplies fluorine for hydrogen fluoride and deuterium fluoride lasers. Nitrogen trifluoride is an extremely potent greenhouse gas, with a 100-year global warming potential about 17,200 times that of CO₂ and an atmospheric lifetime of roughly 740 years. Breathing it irritates the lungs and can cause methemoglobinemia." },
  "PF₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_trifluoride",
    about: "Phosphorus trifluoride is a colorless, odorless gas. Chemists value it mainly as a ligand: it bonds to transition metals much as carbon monoxide does in metal carbonyls, and it can even form complexes, such as Pd(PF₃)₄, whose carbonyl counterparts are not stable. Compared with phosphorus trichloride it is much less sensitive to water, although it does hydrolyze, especially in basic solution, and it attacks glass only when hot. That relative stability means it can be dried over solid potassium hydroxide. Phosphorus trifluoride is highly toxic, with a hazard often compared to phosgene. Like carbon monoxide, it binds strongly to the iron in hemoglobin and blocks the blood from carrying oxygen, and because it has no smell a leak cannot be noticed by odor." },
  "PCl₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_trichloride",
    about: "Phosphorus trichloride is a colorless liquid when pure that fumes in moist air. It reacts vigorously with water to form phosphorous acid and hydrochloric acid, which is why an open container gives off acrid, HCl-smelling fumes. It is made continuously by passing chlorine over white phosphorus, and world production is over a third of a million tonnes a year. Phosphorus trichloride is one of the most important phosphorus chemicals because it is the starting point for phosphorus oxychloride, phosphorus pentachloride, phosphite esters and triphenylphosphine. Those products go on to make herbicides such as glyphosate, insecticides, plasticizers, flame retardants and oil additives. It is highly toxic and corrosive; the U.S. workplace limit is only 0.5 ppm, and it must be kept away from water and moisture." },
  "PBr₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_tribromide",
    about: "Phosphorus tribromide is a dense, colorless liquid that boils at about 173 °C. It has a penetrating odor and fumes in moist air because it hydrolyzes to phosphorous acid and hydrogen bromide. In organic chemistry it is a standard reagent for turning primary and secondary alcohols into alkyl bromides, and it is used this way in making pharmaceuticals such as fenoprofen and alprazolam. It has also been sold as a fire-suppression agent under the name PhostrEx. Phosphorus tribromide is corrosive and causes severe skin and eye burns. It reacts violently with water and alcohols, releasing toxic, corrosive HBr gas, so it should be handled in a fume hood with dry glassware and protective gear." },
  "AsF₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsenic_trifluoride",
    about: "Arsenic trifluoride is a colorless, oily liquid at room temperature; it melts at about −8.5 °C and boils at about 60 °C. Its main role is as a fluorinating agent, swapping chlorine atoms on nonmetal chlorides for fluorine, although it is milder in this job than antimony trifluoride. It reacts readily with water, so it has to be stored and handled under dry conditions. Like other inorganic arsenic compounds, arsenic trifluoride is highly toxic and is classified as dangerous if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed through the skin. U.S. occupational guidelines limit airborne arsenic exposure to extremely small amounts, around 0.010 mg per cubic meter, which reflects how hazardous this compound is to work with." },
  "AsCl₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsenic_trichloride",
    about: "Arsenic trichloride is a colorless, oily liquid (impure samples look yellowish) that boils at about 130 °C. It is sensitive to moisture and fumes in damp air, hydrolyzing to arsenous acid and hydrochloric acid. Its main use is as an intermediate for making organoarsenic compounds, such as triphenylarsine. It also has a dark history: during World War I it was a component of the French agent vincennite, and adding it to acetylene produces lewisite, a blister-agent chemical weapon. Arsenic trichloride is very toxic, corrosive and carcinogenic, and its volatility makes inhalation a serious danger. In the United States it is listed as an extremely hazardous substance, and it should only be handled with full protective equipment and good ventilation." },
  "ClO₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_chlorate",
    about: "Chlorate is an oxyanion in which chlorine has an oxidation state of +5; its salts are the salts of chloric acid. Common examples are sodium chlorate, a white, water-soluble crystalline solid, and potassium chlorate. Chlorate also occurs naturally, with deposits found in dry desert regions, and magnesium chlorate has even been detected on Mars. Sodium chlorate is the key commercial chlorate: nearly all of it is used to make chlorine dioxide for bleaching paper pulp. It has also been used as a nonselective weedkiller and is the oxygen source in the chemical oxygen generators of commercial aircraft. Chlorates were once common in pyrotechnics but have largely given way to perchlorates. Safety note: chlorates are strong oxidizers that deflagrate readily when mixed with combustible materials, and a few grams can be lethal if swallowed." },
  "IO₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iodate",
    about: "Iodate is an oxyanion of iodine in the +5 oxidation state. It is most familiar as potassium iodate, a salt added to table salt to make iodized salt, which helps prevent iodine deficiency. Potassium iodate is also used to help block the thyroid from taking up radioactive iodine, and it is a classic ingredient of the iodine clock reaction demonstration. In acidic solution iodate acts as an oxidizing agent, converting iodide ions into iodine. Iodate minerals such as lautarite and brüggenite are concentrated in the caliche deposits of Chile, where calcium iodate is the main commercial ore for iodine. In natural waters, iodate is the second most abundant dissolved form of iodine and is most common in alkaline, oxidizing conditions." },
  "H₂S": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen_sulfide",
    about: "Hydrogen sulfide is a colorless, flammable, toxic gas famous for its rotten-egg smell. It is released by volcanoes and found in natural gas and petroleum deposits, and it forms wherever sulfate-reducing bacteria break down matter without oxygen, such as in swamps and sewers. Oil and gas processing removes it with the Claus process, which converts it into elemental sulfur, and it has also been used in the Girdler sulfide process for concentrating heavy water. Surprisingly, the human body makes small amounts of hydrogen sulfide and uses it as a signaling molecule, for example in relaxing blood vessels. At higher levels it is highly toxic because it blocks cellular respiration. It is especially dangerous because it quickly deadens the sense of smell, so people may not notice rising exposure, and it is flammable between about 4 and 46 percent in air." },
  "H₂Se": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen_selenide",
    about: "Hydrogen selenide is a colorless, flammable gas at standard conditions and is considered the most toxic selenium compound. At low concentrations it smells like decaying horseradish or rotting garlic, and at higher concentrations its odor resembles rotten eggs. Industrially it is made by heating selenium with hydrogen above 300 °C, while laboratories often prepare it by adding water to aluminum selenide. It is used to dope semiconductors with selenium, to synthesize selenium-containing organic compounds such as selenoureas, and as a route to very pure elemental selenium. Exposure limits are extremely low, about 0.05 ppm averaged over a work shift. Concentrations above about 0.3 ppm irritate the eyes and mucous membranes, and around 1 ppm becomes intolerable, so it demands careful gas handling and ventilation." },
  "H₂Te": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen_telluride",
    about: "Hydrogen telluride is the hydride of tellurium, a colorless gas under standard conditions. Its smell is unpleasant, like rotting garlic at extremely low concentrations and rotting leeks at higher ones. The compound is unstable: it is endothermic and gradually breaks down into hydrogen and tellurium at room temperature, light speeds up that decomposition, and in air it oxidizes to water and elemental tellurium. In water it is a surprisingly strong acid for a hydride, almost as acidic as phosphoric acid. It can be made by electrolytic methods or by treating metal tellurides, such as aluminum telluride, with water. Hydrogen telluride has no significant commercial uses and is mainly a laboratory compound. It is highly toxic and flammable, carrying the top NFPA rating for both health and flammability hazards." },
  "OF₂": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxygen_difluoride",
    about: "Oxygen difluoride is a colorless gas with a peculiar, foul odor; it condenses to a pale yellow liquid and freezes to a white solid. It is unusual because the oxygen atom carries a +2 oxidation state, because it is bonded to fluorine. Boiling at about −145 °C, it is the most volatile triatomic compound that can be isolated. It is prepared by bubbling fluorine gas through dilute sodium hydroxide solution. Because it is such a strong oxidizer, it has attracted interest in rocketry. With water it reacts to form hydrofluoric acid and oxygen. Oxygen difluoride is highly toxic: breathing it can cause pulmonary edema, bleeding in the lungs, headaches, and burns to the skin and eyes, and workplace exposure is limited to just 0.05 ppm." },
  "SCl₂": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_dichloride",
    about: "Sulfur dichloride is a cherry-red liquid with a pungent smell. It is not very stable: at room temperature it slowly loses chlorine and turns into disulfur dichloride, so pure samples are stored in sealed glass ampules with some chlorine gas present. It boils at about 59 °C with decomposition. Chemists use it as a building block for organosulfur compounds, since it reacts with alkenes, ammonia, amines and many other reagents. A well-known and grim example is its reaction with ethylene, a tested route to the chemical weapon sulfur mustard (mustard gas). Sulfur dichloride hydrolyzes in water, releasing hydrogen chloride. It is highly corrosive and toxic, so it must be handled in a fume hood with protective equipment and kept away from moisture." },
  "SBr₂": { state: "unstable", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_dibromide",
    about: "Sulfur dibromide is a sulfur halide that is known only as a short-lived, highly unstable species. It quickly decomposes into disulfur dibromide (S₂Br₂) and elemental bromine, so it cannot be isolated as a pure substance under normal conditions; when chemists try to make it by combining sulfur and bromine, they get the more stable S₂Br₂ instead. It can be generated by treating sulfur dichloride with hydrogen bromide, and it is described as a toxic gas with a foul odor likened to burning rubber. Like sulfur dichloride, it hydrolyzes in water, giving hydrogen bromide, sulfur dioxide and sulfur. Sulfur dibromide has no established practical uses and is mainly of research interest. Any work producing it should be done in a fume hood because of its toxicity and corrosive breakdown products." },
  "TeCl₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tellurium_dichloride",
    about: "Tellurium dichloride is a black solid at room temperature that melts at about 208 °C to a black liquid and boils at about 328 °C, forming a purple vapor. It can be made by reacting tellurium with dichlorodifluoromethane or by combining tellurium with tellurium tetrachloride. The compound is unstable with respect to disproportionation, meaning it tends to convert into tellurium compounds with higher and lower oxidation states. It is only lightly characterized and has attracted little attention, so it has no significant practical uses; its best-studied forms are molecular adducts, such as complexes with thiourea. Tellurium dichloride reacts with water and with diethyl ether, so it must be stored and handled under dry conditions, and it is mainly a laboratory material." },
  "PCl₅": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_pentachloride",
    about: "Phosphorus pentachloride is a colorless, water-sensitive solid, though commercial samples are often yellowish and contaminated with hydrogen chloride. Its structure depends on its state: in the vapor it exists as individual PCl₅ molecules, but the solid is an ionic lattice made of [PCl₄]⁺ cations and [PCl₆]⁻ anions. It is one of the most important chlorinating reagents in synthetic chemistry, used for example to convert carboxylic acids into acyl chlorides. It is also a precursor to lithium hexafluorophosphate, a key electrolyte salt in lithium-ion batteries. Phosphorus pentachloride reacts violently with water, releasing hydrogen chloride and ultimately forming phosphoric acid. It is corrosive to skin, can be fatal if inhaled, and should be handled in a fume hood with protection from moisture." },
  "PF₅": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_pentafluoride",
    about: "Phosphorus pentafluoride is a colorless, toxic gas that fumes in air and has an unpleasant odor; it boils at about −85 °C. It is a Lewis acid, readily forming adducts with electron-pair donors such as pyridine and amines, and this acid behavior is also why it hydrolyzes so easily in moist air. It reacts with hydrogen fluoride to give hexafluorophosphoric acid, the source of the hexafluorophosphate anion, PF₆⁻, a weakly coordinating ion that is widely used in chemistry. The most important hexafluorophosphate is lithium hexafluorophosphate, the salt that carries charge in the electrolytes of lithium-ion batteries and also helps protect their aluminum current collectors. Phosphorus pentafluoride is toxic and reacts with water, so it has to be handled in dry, well-ventilated systems." },
  "PBr₅": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_pentabromide",
    about: "Phosphorus pentabromide is a yellow crystalline solid at room temperature. Despite its formula, the solid is ionic, built from tetrahedral [PBr₄]⁺ cations and bromide ions, so it is more accurately called tetrabromophosphonium bromide; a discrete PBr₅ molecule is not what you find in the crystal. When heated, its vapor dissociates into phosphorus tribromide and bromine. In organic synthesis it serves as a brominating agent and a convenient source of bromine, for example to convert carboxylic acids into acyl bromides. Phosphorus pentabromide reacts with water, and it causes severe skin burns and eye damage, carrying a GHS Danger signal word. It should be stored dry and handled in a fume hood with gloves and eye protection." },
  "AsF₅": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsenic_pentafluoride",
    about: "Arsenic pentafluoride is a toxic, colorless gas that boils at about −53 °C. It can be made by reacting arsenic directly with fluorine, or by fluorinating arsenic trifluoride or arsenic oxides. Chemically it is a very strong fluoride acceptor: it pulls fluoride ions away from other molecules to form hexafluoroarsenate (AsF₆⁻) salts, for example turning sulfur tetrafluoride into an SF₃⁺ salt. This oxidizing, Lewis acidic behavior made it famous in materials research, where doping polyacetylene with arsenic pentafluoride raised the plastic's electrical conductivity close to that of copper, a milestone in the discovery of conducting polymers. Arsenic pentafluoride is highly toxic and a health hazard under GHS labeling, and exposure limits for it are extremely strict, so it is handled only in sealed, specialized equipment." },
  "SbCl₅": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antimony_pentachloride",
    about: "Antimony pentachloride is a chloride of antimony in its +5 oxidation state. At room temperature it is a colorless, oily liquid, though samples usually look yellowish because they contain dissolved chlorine, and it has a sharp, unpleasant smell. It melts at about 3 °C and starts to decompose above roughly 106 °C, before its nominal boiling point of 140 °C. Chemists use it as a catalyst for polymerization reactions and for adding chlorine to organic compounds, and it serves as a reference Lewis acid when comparing how strongly different molecules donate electron pairs. Safety note: it is highly corrosive and reacts with water or moist air to release hydrogen chloride, which is why it is stored only in glass or PTFE containers." },
  "SbF₅": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antimony_pentafluoride",
    about: "Antimony pentafluoride is an inorganic fluoride of antimony and one of the strongest Lewis acids known. At room temperature it is a colorless, thick, oily liquid with a pungent odor that readily absorbs moisture from the air; it melts at 8.3 °C and boils near 150 °C. Its best-known role is in superacids: mixing liquid SbF₅ with liquid hydrogen fluoride produces fluoroantimonic acid, an extraordinarily strong acid that attacks almost every known substance. Chemists use such superacid media to generate and study highly reactive positive ions. Safety note: SbF₅ is extremely toxic and corrosive, acts as an oxidizer, and reacts violently with water, often releasing dangerous hydrogen fluoride, so it is handled only with specialized equipment." },
  "NbCl₅": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niobium(V)_chloride",
    about: "Niobium pentachloride, also called niobium(V) chloride, is the main chloride of the metal niobium. At room temperature it is a yellow crystalline solid that pulls moisture from the air and melts at about 205 °C. The single NbCl₅ molecule exists as a discrete unit in the gas phase; in the solid, pairs of molecules are joined by shared chlorine bridges to form dimers, Nb₂Cl₁₀. It is the chief starting material for other niobium compounds, especially niobium alkoxides used in sol-gel processing to make oxide materials, and it serves as a Lewis acid catalyst in organic reactions such as Diels-Alder reactions. Safety note: it is corrosive, irritates skin and airways, and hydrolyzes in moist air." },
  "SF₄": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_tetrafluoride",
    about: "Sulfur tetrafluoride is a compound of sulfur and fluorine used mainly as a reagent in organic synthesis. At room temperature it is a colorless gas that boils at −38 °C and is easily compressed into a liquid under pressure. Its main job is replacing oxygen with fluorine: it converts carboxylic acid groups and carbonyl groups (C=O) into fluorinated groups, a step used to make organofluorine compounds for pharmaceuticals and specialty chemicals. In many laboratories it has been replaced by safer liquid reagents such as diethylaminosulfur trifluoride (DAST). Safety note: SF₄ is very dangerous to inhale, because it reacts with moisture, including moisture in the lungs, to form sulfur dioxide and hydrogen fluoride, which becomes highly toxic, corrosive hydrofluoric acid." },
  "SeF₄": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selenium_tetrafluoride",
    about: "Selenium tetrafluoride is an inorganic compound of selenium and fluorine. At room temperature it is a colorless liquid, and it stays liquid over a wide range, from about −13 °C up to its boiling point of about 101 °C. Its main use is as a fluorinating reagent in organic synthesis, where it converts alcohols, carboxylic acids, and carbonyl compounds into their fluorinated counterparts. Compared with the related gas sulfur tetrafluoride, it can do this under milder conditions. Safety note: it reacts readily with water, and it is rated as a significant health hazard, so it must be stored dry and handled with care in a fume hood." },
  "TeF₄": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tellurium_tetrafluoride",
    about: "Tellurium tetrafluoride is one of only two known simple binary fluorides of tellurium, the other being tellurium hexafluoride. At room temperature it is a stable, white crystalline solid that absorbs moisture from the air. It melts at about 130 °C and decomposes near 194 °C, forming tellurium hexafluoride. The isolated four-fluorine molecule is best thought of as a gas-phase species: in the solid, TeF₄ units link through shared fluorine atoms into long chains. It is mainly a laboratory compound, commonly made by reacting tellurium dioxide with sulfur tetrafluoride, and it dissolves in antimony pentafluoride. Handling note: it reacts with water and with silica (glass) to form tellurium oxides, and it attacks metals such as copper and nickel when heated." },
  "ClF₃": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chlorine_trifluoride",
    about: "Chlorine trifluoride is an interhalogen compound, meaning it is made of two different halogens, and it is one of the most aggressive oxidizers known. It boils at about 12 °C, so at room temperature it is a colorless gas, though it is usually shipped as a greenish-yellow liquid under pressure. The semiconductor industry uses it to clean deposition chambers, and it is used in nuclear fuel processing to convert uranium into uranium hexafluoride. It was also studied as a high-performance rocket oxidizer. Safety note: ClF₃ is toxic and corrosive, sets many materials on fire on contact without any ignition source, and reacts explosively with water to form hydrofluoric and hydrochloric acids; its fires are extremely hard to put out." },
  "BrF₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromine_trifluoride",
    about: "Bromine trifluoride is an interhalogen compound of bromine and fluorine. At room temperature it is a straw-colored liquid with a choking, pungent smell; it melts near 9 °C and boils near 126 °C. It is a powerful fluorinating agent and is used in nuclear fuel processing and reprocessing to produce uranium hexafluoride. Chemists also use liquid BrF₃ as an unusual solvent: it conducts electricity because a small fraction of it splits into BrF₂⁺ and BrF₄⁻ ions, a process called self-ionization. Safety note: it is toxic, corrosive, and a strong oxidizer, and it reacts very exothermically with water to form hydrobromic acid, hydrofluoric acid, and oxygen, so it must be kept away from moisture." },
  "IF₃": { state: "unstable", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iodine_trifluoride",
    about: "Iodine trifluoride is an interhalogen compound of iodine and fluorine. It is a yellow solid, but only at low temperatures: it decomposes above about −28 °C, so it cannot exist as a stable substance at room temperature. Because it is so unstable, relatively little is known about it, and it has no practical applications; it is essentially a laboratory curiosity. Chemists prepare it at very low temperature, for example by reacting iodine with fluorine at −45 °C in a solvent, or by reacting iodine with xenon difluoride, taking care not to form iodine pentafluoride instead." },
  "XeF₂": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenon_difluoride",
    about: "Xenon difluoride is one of the most stable compounds of the noble gas xenon. It was first reported in 1962, the same year chemists showed that noble gases could form compounds at all. At room temperature it is a dense, colorless crystalline solid with a nauseating odor and a low vapor pressure; it melts at about 129 °C. It is a powerful but controllable fluorinating agent used to add fluorine atoms to organic molecules. In microelectronics it is used as a gaseous etchant for silicon, especially when making microelectromechanical systems (MEMS), tiny machines built on chips. Safety note: it is corrosive to skin and eyes and releases toxic compounds on contact with moisture, so it must be kept dry." },
  "I₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triiodide",
    about: "The triiodide ion is a polyhalogen ion made of three iodine atoms carrying one negative charge. It forms whenever iodine (I₂) dissolves in a solution containing iodide ions (I⁻), such as potassium iodide solution, through the equilibrium I₂ + I⁻ ⇌ I₃⁻. Its solutions range from yellow when dilute to brown or red when concentrated. Triiodide is the main iodine species in Lugol's iodine, a mixture of iodine and potassium iodide in water, and it is also present in tincture of iodine. It is responsible for the familiar blue-black color in the starch test. Solid salts such as ammonium triiodide are known, and the triiodide/iodide pair is used in dye-sensitized solar cells and some rechargeable batteries." },
  "ICl₂⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyhalogen_ions",
    about: "The dichloroiodate ion is a polyhalogen anion in which one iodine atom is bonded to two chlorine atoms, with an overall charge of −1. It can be made by passing chlorine into a solution of potassium iodide, giving salts such as potassium dichloroiodate (KICl₂), and its compounds are typically orange to yellow. Like other polyhalogen anions, it is stabilized in solids by the lattice energy of its salts, and on heating such salts tend to break down into a simple chloride plus an interhalogen." },
  "SF₆": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_hexafluoride",
    about: "Sulfur hexafluoride is a colorless, odorless, nonflammable gas that is about five times denser than air and very unreactive. Most SF₆ produced is used in the electrical power industry as an insulating gas in high-voltage circuit breakers and gas-insulated switchgear. It also has medical uses: tiny SF₆ microbubbles serve as an ultrasound contrast agent, and the gas is used in some retinal detachment surgeries. It was once widely used as a protective cover gas in magnesium casting. Environmental note: SF₆ is an extremely potent greenhouse gas, trapping about 23,500 times more heat than carbon dioxide over 100 years, and it lasts in the atmosphere for roughly 800 to 3,200 years. Safety note: though nontoxic, it can cause suffocation by displacing oxygen." },
  "SeF₆": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selenium_hexafluoride",
    about: "Selenium hexafluoride is an inorganic compound of selenium in its +6 oxidation state bonded to six fluorine atoms. At room temperature it is a colorless gas with a repulsive odor; it sublimes, passing directly from solid to gas, at about −34.5 °C. Unlike its well-known relative sulfur hexafluoride, it has no commercial applications and is rarely encountered outside research laboratories. It is fairly resistant to water and can even pass through dilute sodium hydroxide solution unchanged, although it is more reactive than SF₆ and reacts with ammonia gas when heated. Safety note: it is highly toxic; workplace exposure limits are set at just 0.05 ppm, and 2 ppm is considered immediately dangerous to life and health." },
  "TeF₆": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tellurium_hexafluoride",
    about: "Tellurium hexafluoride is a compound of tellurium in its +6 oxidation state with six fluorine atoms. At room temperature it is a colorless gas with a repulsive odor; it turns into a volatile white solid below about −38 °C. It can be made by heating tellurium with fluorine gas at about 150 °C, or by treating tellurium trioxide with bromine trifluoride. Unlike sulfur hexafluoride, it has no major industrial uses and is mainly studied in the laboratory. It is also more reactive than SF₆: in water it slowly hydrolyzes to telluric acid, Te(OH)₆, releasing hydrofluoric acid. Safety note: it is highly toxic, with a U.S. workplace exposure limit of only 0.02 ppm and an immediately dangerous level of 1 ppm." },
  "MoF₆": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molybdenum_hexafluoride",
    about: "Molybdenum hexafluoride is an inorganic compound of molybdenum in its highest oxidation state, +6. It melts at 17.5 °C and boils at 34 °C, so at about 25 °C it is a colorless liquid that can freeze into a white solid on a cool day and evaporates easily. It absorbs moisture from the air. The semiconductor industry uses it in chemical vapor deposition, a process for laying down thin molybdenum layers while building integrated circuits. It also turns up in nuclear technology as an impurity in uranium hexafluoride, since molybdenum is one of the elements formed when uranium splits. Handling note: it hydrolyzes easily when it meets water, forming impurities such as molybdenum oxyfluorides, so it must be kept strictly dry." },
  "WF₆": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tungsten_hexafluoride",
    about: "Tungsten hexafluoride is an inorganic compound of tungsten and fluorine. It boils at about 17 °C, so at room temperature it is a colorless gas; it is the densest known gas at ordinary conditions, roughly 11 times heavier than air. Below 17 °C it condenses to a colorless liquid, and below about 2 °C it freezes to a white solid. Its main use is in the semiconductor industry, where chemical vapor deposition turns WF₆ into thin films of tungsten metal that form low-resistance wiring inside microchips; worldwide use is around 200 tonnes per year. Safety note: WF₆ is toxic and corrosive, reacting with water to form hydrogen fluoride and tungsten oxyfluorides, so it is stored in equipment with Teflon seals." },
  "UF₆": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uranium_hexafluoride",
    about: "Uranium hexafluoride is a compound of uranium and fluorine that is central to making nuclear fuel. At room temperature it is a colorless solid, but it is very volatile and sublimes, turning straight into a gas, at 56.5 °C under normal pressure. That easy conversion to a gas is why it is used to enrich uranium: UF₆ gas is fed into gas centrifuges, or formerly gaseous diffusion plants, to separate the lighter uranium-235 from uranium-238. Safety note: UF₆ is toxic, corrosive, and radioactive. It reacts with moisture in the air to form uranyl fluoride and hydrogen fluoride, both highly corrosive and toxic, and large releases have caused deaths." },
  "PF₆⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexafluorophosphate",
    about: "The hexafluorophosphate ion is a phosphorus atom surrounded by six fluorine atoms with an overall charge of −1. Its salts are colorless, and the ion is known as a weakly coordinating anion, meaning it rarely bonds to metal ions or reacts with other species, so chemists use it as a spectator counterion. Its most important salt is lithium hexafluorophosphate (LiPF₆), a common electrolyte in commercial lithium-ion batteries because it dissolves well in organic solvents. It is also part of ionic liquids such as bmimPF₆, and ammonium hexafluorophosphate is used to prepare other PF₆⁻ salts. The ion resists hydrolysis, but it can slowly break down and release hydrogen fluoride in some conditions, and making its salts involves handling hazardous HF." },
  "SiF₆²⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexafluorosilicic_acid",
    about: "The hexafluorosilicate ion is a silicon atom surrounded by six fluorine atoms, carrying a −2 charge. It occurs in hexafluorosilicic acid (H₂SiF₆), which is produced in large amounts as a byproduct of making phosphate fertilizers, and in salts such as sodium, potassium, ammonium, and barium hexafluorosilicate. The acid and sodium hexafluorosilicate are widely used to add fluoride to drinking water. Most of the acid is converted into aluminum fluoride and synthetic cryolite for aluminum production, and fluorosilicates are also used in lead refining, concrete hardening, wood preservation, and rust removers. In water the ion slowly breaks down, releasing fluoride ions and silica. Safety note: hexafluorosilicic acid is toxic if swallowed, attacks glass, and can release hydrogen fluoride." },
  "BrF₅": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromine_pentafluoride",
    about: "Bromine pentafluoride is an interhalogen compound of bromine and fluorine and a very strong fluorinating agent. At room temperature it is a pale yellow liquid that boils at about 40 °C, so it evaporates readily. It is used in geochemistry for oxygen isotope analysis, where it reacts with silicate minerals (often heated by a laser) to release their oxygen for measurement. It has also been tested as an oxidizer in liquid rocket propellants and is used to fluorinate uranium during nuclear fuel processing. Safety note: BrF₅ is severely corrosive and toxic, and its vapors irritate the eyes, skin, and lungs. It reacts violently with water to form hydrofluoric and bromic acids, and it can ignite flammable materials on contact." },
  "IF₅": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iodine_pentafluoride",
    about: "Iodine pentafluoride is an interhalogen compound of iodine and fluorine, first made by French chemist Henri Moissan in 1891 by burning iodine in fluorine gas, which is still the basic way it is produced. At room temperature it is a colorless liquid, though impure samples look yellow; it freezes near 9 °C and boils near 98 °C. It is used as a fluorinating reagent in chemical synthesis, adding fluorine atoms to other compounds, and as a specialty solvent for handling metal fluorides. Safety note: IF₅ is toxic, corrosive, and an oxidizer. It reacts vigorously with water to form hydrofluoric acid and iodic acid, so contact with moisture or skin is especially dangerous and it must be handled in dry, sealed equipment." },
  "ClF₅": { state: "gas", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chlorine_pentafluoride",
    about: "Chlorine pentafluoride is an interhalogen compound of chlorine and fluorine and a very strong oxidizer. At room temperature it is a colorless gas with a sweet odor that boils at about −13 °C. It was first made in 1963, and some early research on how to prepare it was classified. It was studied as an oxidizer for rocket propellants, since it could deliver more thrust than chlorine trifluoride, but it was never used in large systems because of its hazards and the large amounts of hydrogen fluoride in its exhaust. Safety note: ClF₅ reacts violently with water, moist air, and even ice at −100 °C, producing hydrofluoric acid and chlorine gas, and it can be fatal if inhaled." },
  "XeF₄": { state: "solid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenon_tetrafluoride",
    about: "Xenon tetrafluoride is a compound of the noble gas xenon with four fluorine atoms. It was discovered in 1962, soon after Neil Bartlett showed that xenon could form chemical compounds, and it was the first binary compound of a noble gas to be found. At room temperature it is a colorless crystalline solid that sublimes, turning directly into vapor, at about 117 °C. It is made by heating xenon with excess fluorine in a nickel vessel at about 400 °C. One practical use is analyzing silicone rubber: XeF₄ breaks down the rubber and leaves behind trace metal impurities for measurement. Safety note: it hydrolyzes in water to form xenon, oxygen, hydrofluoric acid, and xenon trioxide, so it must be kept dry." },
  "ICl₄⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrachloroiodic_acid",
    about: "The tetrachloroiodate ion is an iodine atom in the +3 oxidation state bonded to four chlorine atoms, with an overall charge of −1. It forms when iodine trichloride reacts with chloride ions, for example when ICl₃ dissolves in concentrated hydrochloric acid to give tetrachloroiodic acid, HICl₄, or when it combines with potassium chloride or potassium iodide. Its salts are golden-yellow to orange; potassium tetrachloroiodate forms yellow crystals, and tetrachloroiodic acid exists as orange crystals that are unstable in air. The lighter alkali-metal salts slowly lose iodine trichloride, while the cesium salt is stable at room temperature. These compounds are mainly of research interest. Handling note: they release iodine trichloride, a strong oxidizer that can ignite organic materials." },
  "BrF₄⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyhalogen_ions",
    about: "The tetrafluorobromate ion is a bromine atom in the +3 oxidation state bonded to four fluorine atoms, with an overall charge of −1. It is found in liquid bromine trifluoride, where a small fraction of BrF₃ molecules split into BrF₂⁺ and BrF₄⁻ ions; this self-ionization is why liquid BrF₃ conducts electricity. Solid salts such as potassium tetrafluorobromate (KBrF₄) can be made by treating potassium chloride with excess bromine trifluoride. As with other polyhalogen anions, large cations such as cesium help stabilize these salts, and on decomposition they tend to break down into a simple fluoride plus an interhalogen. Polyhalogen fluoride anions are generally weaker oxidants than the related cations. Handling note: BrF₄⁻ salts are tied to BrF₃, a toxic, corrosive, water-reactive oxidizer." },
  "XeCl₄": { state: "unstable", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenon_tetrachloride",
    about: "Xenon tetrachloride is a compound of the noble gas xenon that, unlike xenon tetrafluoride, cannot be kept as a stable substance. It has been made in tiny amounts, for example by passing an electric discharge through xenon and chlorine, or by the radioactive decay of iodine-129 in a tetrachloroiodate salt, which turns the iodine atom into xenon in place. Chemists identified the square planar product with Mössbauer spectroscopy rather than by isolating it in a bottle. It readily falls apart into xenon and chlorine. Because chlorine is less electronegative than fluorine, it holds xenon's electrons less firmly, which is a useful way to see why xenon fluorides are far more stable than xenon chlorides." },
  "H₃O⁺": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydronium",
    about: "The hydronium ion forms when a water molecule accepts a proton (H⁺). Every acid dissolved in water raises the hydronium concentration, and the pH scale is a measure of how much hydronium is present: lower pH means more hydronium. Pure water also contains a little hydronium, because water molecules occasionally pass a proton to one another, leaving equal amounts of hydronium and hydroxide. In real solutions the ion is surrounded by hydrogen-bonded water molecules, forming larger clusters such as the Zundel and Eigen cations, and protons move quickly by hopping from one water molecule to the next. Hydronium salts can also be crystallized with strong acids, and the ion has been detected in interstellar space and comet tails." },
  "SO₃²⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfite",
    about: "The sulfite ion is formed when sulfur dioxide dissolves in water and reacts with a base; it is the fully deprotonated form of sulfurous acid. Sulfite salts such as sodium sulfite and sodium metabisulfite are widely used as food and wine preservatives and antioxidants, where they slow browning and stop unwanted microbes. Wine labels in many countries state contains sulfites when the level passes a small threshold. Sulfites are also used to remove dissolved oxygen from boiler water, in paper pulping, and in photographic developers. They are mild reducing agents and are slowly oxidized by air to sulfate. Safety: some people, especially those with asthma, are sensitive to sulfites in food and can experience breathing difficulty." },
  "BrO₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromate",
    about: "The bromate ion is a bromine oxyanion and a strong oxidizing agent. Potassium bromate was once common as a flour improver that strengthens bread dough, but it is banned for that use in many countries because it is classified as a possible human carcinogen. Bromate also matters in drinking water: when water that naturally contains bromide is disinfected with ozone, some bromide is oxidized to bromate, so water regulators set a low limit for it, such as 10 micrograms per liter in the United States and European Union. In the laboratory, bromate is used in titrations and in oscillating reactions such as the Belousov–Zhabotinsky reaction, which cycles through colors." },
  "PCl₄⁺": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_pentachloride",
    about: "The tetrachlorophosphonium ion is best known from solid phosphorus pentachloride. As a gas or in non-polar solvents, PCl₅ exists as trigonal bipyramidal molecules, but in the solid it rearranges into an ionic salt made of tetrahedral PCl₄⁺ cations and octahedral PCl₆⁻ anions. This change is a striking example of how the same formula can adopt different shapes depending on its surroundings. The ion also forms when PCl₅ reacts with strong chloride acceptors such as aluminum chloride or antimony pentachloride. Because its four bonds point to the corners of a tetrahedron, it is isoelectronic and isostructural with silicon tetrachloride, which makes it a handy comparison when learning VSEPR theory." },
  "NO₂⁺": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitronium_ion",
    about: "The nitronium ion is the reactive species behind aromatic nitration, one of the most important reactions in organic chemistry. It forms when concentrated nitric acid is mixed with concentrated sulfuric acid: the sulfuric acid protonates nitric acid, which then loses water to give NO₂⁺. The ion attacks benzene rings to make nitro compounds, which are used to produce dyes, medicines, and explosives such as TNT. Nitronium is isoelectronic with carbon dioxide, so it shares the same straight, linear shape. It can also be isolated in stable salts with weakly basic anions, such as nitronium tetrafluoroborate, which chemists use as a convenient nitrating agent." },
  "NH₂⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_amide",
    about: "The amide ion is the conjugate base of ammonia, formed by removing one of its protons. Because ammonia is an extremely weak acid, the amide ion is an extremely strong base. It is most often handled as sodium amide, a white solid made by reacting sodium metal with ammonia, usually with an iron catalyst. Chemists use sodium amide to remove protons in organic synthesis, for example to make alkynes from dihalides or to form carbanions. In liquid ammonia, the amide ion plays a role similar to hydroxide in water. Safety: sodium amide reacts violently with water, releasing ammonia and heat, and old samples can form explosive peroxides, so it is stored dry and handled with care." },
  "ClO₂⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chlorite",
    about: "The chlorite ion is a chlorine oxyanion in which chlorine has an oxidation state of +3. Its most important compound is sodium chlorite, which is used to generate chlorine dioxide for bleaching paper pulp and textiles and for disinfecting drinking water. Chlorite is also produced as a by-product when water is treated with chlorine dioxide, so drinking-water regulators limit how much may remain, for example 1.0 milligram per liter in the United States. Chlorite salts are oxidizers that can react dangerously with combustible materials and acids. Its bent shape comes from two lone pairs on chlorine, which makes it a useful comparison with chlorate and perchlorate, the ions with one and zero lone pairs." },
  "SCl₄": { state: "unstable", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur_tetrachloride",
    about: "Sulfur tetrachloride is a pale yellow solid that exists only at low temperature. It is made by chlorinating sulfur dichloride below about −30 °C, and above roughly that temperature it decomposes back into sulfur dichloride and chlorine. In the solid state it is better described as an ionic salt, SCl₃⁺Cl⁻, rather than as discrete molecules. It reacts with water to form sulfur dioxide and hydrochloric acid. The molecule predicted by VSEPR theory has a seesaw shape, just like its stable fluorine analogue sulfur tetrafluoride. Comparing the two shows how a more electronegative outer atom, fluorine, stabilizes high oxidation states much better than chlorine can." },
  "ICl₄⁺": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyhalogen_ions",
    about: "The tetrachloroiodonium ion is a polyhalogen cation in which a central iodine atom is bonded to four chlorine atoms. It forms when iodine trichloride gives up a chloride ion to a strong chloride acceptor, such as antimony pentachloride or aluminum chloride, and it is found in salts with these large, weakly basic anions. Like other interhalogen cations, it is very reactive and is studied under dry, non-aqueous conditions. Its central iodine has five electron domains, four bonds and one lone pair, so it adopts a seesaw shape. It pairs neatly with the tetrachloroiodate anion, ICl₄⁻, which has one more lone pair and is square planar, making the two a classic VSEPR comparison." },
  "IBr₃": { state: "liquid", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iodine_tribromide",
    about: "Iodine tribromide is an interhalogen compound made from iodine and bromine. It is a dark brown liquid that mixes with ethanol and ethers. Chemists use it as a brominating agent in organic synthesis, and it has been used in the semiconductor industry for dry etching. Like other interhalogens, it is reactive and corrosive, reacting with water and releasing irritating vapors. Its central iodine atom has three bonds and two lone pairs, so VSEPR theory predicts a T-shaped molecule, the same shape as chlorine trifluoride and bromine trifluoride. Comparing these molecules shows how a larger central atom can hold more electron domains, which is why iodine readily forms compounds with more than eight valence electrons around it." },
  "Br₃⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tribromide",
    about: "The tribromide ion forms when a bromide ion combines with a bromine molecule, a reaction that happens readily in solutions containing both. Solutions of bromine in bromide salts are orange because of this ion. Tribromide salts with large cations, such as tetrabutylammonium tribromide and pyridinium tribromide, are crystalline solids that chemists use as safer, easier-to-measure substitutes for liquid bromine when adding bromine to organic molecules. The ion is linear, like triiodide: the central bromine has two bonds and three lone pairs arranged in a trigonal bipyramid, with the lone pairs in the equatorial positions. Safety: tribromide reagents release bromine and are corrosive and irritating." },
  "IF₂⁻": { state: "ion", source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyhalogen_ions",
    about: "The difluoroiodate ion is a polyhalide anion in which a central iodine atom is bonded to two fluorine atoms and carries a negative charge. It belongs to the same family as the triiodide and dichloroiodate ions, which form when a halide ion adds to a halogen or interhalogen molecule. These ions are typically stabilized in salts with large cations, such as tetraalkylammonium ions, and are studied under dry conditions because they react with water. With two bonds and three lone pairs on iodine, the electron geometry is trigonal bipyramidal, and placing the three lone pairs in the equatorial positions leaves a straight, linear ion. It is a standard AX₂E₃ example in VSEPR practice problems." },
};
